Baseline PBR shading, atmosphere and surface detail
==================================================

Scope and shared contracts
  Improve baseline physically based rendering with and without LumOn. Provide a physically based
  sky, optional height/depth-map tessellation and relief mapping, PBR liquids, an investigation of
  approximate underwater caustics, and volumetric clouds.
  Keep standalone PBR usable without LumOn resources or indirect-lighting production. Standalone
  environmental lighting should keep outdoor shadows readable without injecting sunlight through
  occluders or applying an indiscriminate brightness floor to enclosed spaces.
  These are implementation tasks; algorithm choices and performance targets require investigation.
  Reuse existing shader/resource owners, compiled SPIR-V contracts, demand loading and settings.
  Keep optional features bounded, configurable and independently disableable.
  This list owns baseline PBR material improvements. Surface Cache performance
  and geometry work remain in docs/LumOn.SurfaceCache.PerformanceAndMaterials.todo.

Tasks

  ☐ Optimize water rendering and composition performance. See docs/PBR.WaterPerformance.todo.
    - Remove recurring executable preparation, reduce redundant receiver/scattering work, and
      gate unnecessary background capture while preserving the established water rendering contracts.

  Material-definition decision
    Retain shared BRDFProperties schema and C# models for existing material-wide properties.
    Per-axis-face overrides were withdrawn: texture-to-material mappings already let different
    block-face textures use different properties and naturally follow block rotation.

  ☐ Fully replace the base-game sky shader with a VGE-owned shader.
    - Implemented ownership: atmosphere publication supplies physical-model-derived compatibility
      lighting before ambient blending; an independently registered sky IRenderer consumes it.
      A lifecycle prefix suppresses the complete engine sky callback regardless of draw readiness.
      Atmospheric/frame publication is independent of drawing; no per-frame vanilla fallback remains. Shared frame/resource
      inputs have a separate adapter; legacy cloud lookup content remains a compatibility dependency.
      Contract and limits: docs/PBR.Atmosphere.Publication.md.
    - Owned pbr_sky SPIR-V and typed inputs reuse the published atmosphere and primary framebuffer;
      the renderer owns a procedural fullscreen triangle and reconstructs camera-relative rays per pixel. Runtime always emits scene-linear HDR.
      The earlier draw-hook/shader-handoff validation is historical and does not validate the revised
      ownership. User-run visual acceptance remains required before completion.
      Evidence: docs/PBR.Atmosphere.md / Owned fullscreen submission.
    - Persistent ownership validation: Debug build passed; 265 regression tests passed with five
      opt-in measurement skips, no GPU-availability skips. See docs/PBR.Atmosphere.Publication.md.
      Twilight/night compatibility mapping and legacy cloud appearance still require user review.
    - Reuse the existing atmospheric LUT generation, publication and shared lighting inputs;
      replace the engine sky program rather than extending its GLSL with further HDR patches.
    - Use the established VGE shader asset, typed binding, registration and SPIR-V build paths.
      Establish draw ownership while preserving engine sky geometry, render order and celestial
      ordering; avoid duplicate sky draws and keep resource orchestration thin.
    - Explicitly own twilight/star visibility alpha, horizon and liquid-depth masking, underwater
      tint, night vision, psychedelic effects, glow and G-buffer/SSAO outputs. Specify which effects
      remain spatial sky operations and which belong at the final display boundary.
    - Emit scene-linear HDR on prepared HDR frames, retaining the compatible legacy display route
      until the common scene handoff is ready. Preserve values above one; apply display conversion
      and SDR dithering once at the selected boundary. Keep UI/offscreen rendering separate.
    - Enable scene-HDR participation only after the owned program and bindings are
      verified. This task supports full-scene HDR activation but does not gate water Phase 2.
    - Preserve shader reload, resize, world teardown, failure handling and both PBR lighting modes.
      Remove superseded sky source patches when the owned shader assumes their responsibilities.
    - Validate LUT row/seam sampling, day/night alpha and celestial ordering, perception effects,
      above/underwater views, HDR/legacy output and lifecycle with focused subagent tests. Retain
      existing atmospheric-model tests; obtain user-run visual acceptance without launching the game.
    - Contracts: docs/PBR.Atmosphere.md, docs/PBR.SharedDisplay.md, docs/PBR.OutputDithering.md,
      docs/PBR.MaterialColorAndDisplay.md and docs/PBR.WaterRefraction.md.

  ☐ Add optional depth-map tessellation and relief mapping through the production shader pipeline.

  ☐ Try and make tessellation do square subdivision by subdividing faces as quads.

  ☐ Implement scene reflections for PBR liquids.
    - Extend PBR.Liquids.md's current environment approximation with reflected scene radiance.
      Evaluate bounded screen-space reflection tracing against the available scene depth/color;
      specify which geometry is represented and provide an atmosphere/environment fallback for
      offscreen rays, disocclusions, missing depth and unsupported transparent receivers.
    - Use the animated liquid normal, view direction, material roughness and Fresnel response.
      Coordinate with refraction and total internal reflection so reflected/transmitted energy is
      combined once; replace the existing reflection approximation rather than adding it twice.
      Retain distinct material and emission behavior for water, lava and other liquids.
    - Share coherent scene snapshots with refraction where their publication boundaries permit it.
      Avoid active-framebuffer feedback, self-intersections and foreground leakage. Define handling
      of rough reflections, screen edges, underwater views and overlapping transparent surfaces.
    - Provide a persisted Liquid Reflections graphics option and bounded quality settings. When
      disabled, retain the inexpensive environment fallback and skip reflection-only work/resources;
      shared snapshots remain alive only while another enabled consumer needs them.
    - Reuse screen-resource lifecycle and shader ownership for resize, reload and runtime toggling.
      If temporal reconstruction is adopted, reject invalid history on camera/world changes and
      disocclusion. Support both PBR modes without requiring LumOn and coordinate color/exposure
      with the scene-linear HDR task.
    - Validate reflection direction, Fresnel weighting, roughness, fallback transitions, moving
      geometry, shorelines, submerged views and settings/lifecycle changes with focused subagent
      tests. Obtain user-run visual acceptance and measure GPU tracing, filtering, snapshot bandwidth
      and memory costs separately and with refraction enabled.

  ☐ Add a camera-contact waterline/meniscus screen effect at the liquid transition boundary.
    - Follow the active liquid-rendering task immediately. Simulate liquid contacting the camera
      lens when the view straddles the surface: a narrow curved/distorted waterline with restrained
      optical highlights and tint, rather than an abrupt above-water/underwater seam.
    - Derive the screen-space boundary from camera/liquid intersection and the animated local
      surface, not a fixed horizontal screen band or only the camera-underwater boolean. Handle
      partial submersion, camera pitch/roll, waves, flowing surfaces and entering/exiting liquid.
    - Integrate with existing liquid depth, fog and OIT ownership. Keep distortion bounded, avoid
      active-framebuffer feedback and prevent leakage onto unrelated foreground geometry or UI.
      Reuse refraction sampling/resources when available, with a defined fallback when disabled.
    - Keep this localized contact effect distinct from bulk underwater absorption/scattering and
      optional persistent lens droplets. Define suitable behavior for non-water liquids, including
      emissive liquids, instead of applying water highlights indiscriminately.
    - Expose a persisted graphics setting; disabling it must skip effect-specific rendering and
      resources. Support both PBR modes, runtime toggling, resize/reload and stable transitions
      without flicker when the camera or waves repeatedly cross the surface.
    - Validate boundary placement and depth rejection with focused subagent tests, then obtain
      user-run acceptance for slow/fast entry and exit, bobbing, tilted views, shorelines and waves.
      Measure GPU time and any scene-copy bandwidth; document the lens-contact approximation.

  ☐ Explore approximate underwater caustics and decide whether to adopt them.
    - Compare bounded procedural/projected approaches against desired appearance and GPU cost.
      Use water motion, light direction, receiver depth and surface orientation where appropriate.
    - Restrict contributions to submerged receiving surfaces under relevant water and illumination;
      avoid effects above water, through opaque occluders or where the sun is fully shadowed.
    - Evaluate both lighting pipelines, underwater transitions, temporal stability and double-counting
      with existing lighting. Clearly label the effect as an approximation, not traced light transport.
    - Record prototype results and an adoption decision before production implementation. If adopted,
      provide an independent toggle, strength/quality bounds and targeted visual/performance checks.

  ✘ Refactor PBR systems which currently directly use base-game renderbuffer attachments, to instead create a TextureView instance for those attachments and use the texture-view instead. @cancelled(26-10-02 12:33)
    - Verified prerequisite: installed engine primary/shadow attachments are texture-backed but use
      mutable TexImage2D storage. Real glTextureView objects require immutable storage; a headless
      diagnostic reproduced InvalidOperation with no view object created. Evidence:
      artifacts/EngineAttachmentViews/SetupDefaultFrameBuffers.il.txt and mutable-view-diagnostic.log.
    - Resolve whether to preserve engine allocation using non-owning GpuTexture wrappers or change
      engine attachment allocation to immutable storage for real OpenGL texture views.
    - Correct GpuTextureView creation validation: the diagnostic found IsValid reports true after
      rejected creation because it only checks a generated nonzero name.
    - Preserve engine storage ownership, sampling policy and attachment replacement/retirement;
      migrate direct lighting, composite/display resolve, liquids and water-volume inputs together.

  ☐ Implement physically motivated volumetric clouds integrated with the sky.
    - Define bounded 3D cloud density, altitude/weather controls, wind and volume lighting. Evaluate
      ray marching, light transmittance and a controlled multiple-scattering approximation.
    - Integrate cloud/atmosphere ordering, sun occlusion and cloud shadows/environment lighting in
      both PBR modes. Support ground, inside-cloud and above-cloud views without depth-order errors.
    - Add quality-scaled resolution/steps, empty-space skipping and temporal reconstruction where
      useful; invalidate history correctly on movement, weather changes and lighting transitions.
    - Define replacement of vanilla clouds and clean disable/reload behavior. Validate thin/thick
      clouds, moving sun, weather, disocclusion, water reflections and fog; measure GPU and memory cost.

  ☐ Implement industry-standard dynamic camera contrast adjustment for the HDR scene.
    - Implementation contract and remaining visual/performance acceptance: docs/PBR.CameraExposure.md.
      GPU histogram metering and bounded temporal exposure precede the existing stable tone curve;
      lighting/refraction stay unexposed. Persisted automatic/manual controls share one camera model.
    - Investigate established automatic exposure and adaptive tone-mapping approaches; define the
      intended camera response, metering units and contrast behavior before selecting an algorithm.
      Distinguish exposure adaptation from changes to the tone curve; do not substitute independent
      per-frame minimum/maximum normalization for a temporally stable camera model.
    - Meter scene-linear luminance after scene/OIT/late composition and before display conversion.
      Evaluate bounded GPU histogram/percentile or log-luminance reduction with robust rejection of
      isolated solar/emissive peaks and near-black outliers. Exclude UI/offscreen draws and define
      metering relative to bloom/light shafts so generated glare cannot drive an exposure feedback loop.
    - Define exposure compensation, a middle-gray target, minimum/maximum exposure in stops and
      configurable metering behavior. Adapt over elapsed time with independently bounded brightening
      and darkening rates; prevent pumping, flicker and frame-rate-dependent transitions.
    - Define highlight shoulder, shadow toe and midtone contrast while preserving color ratios,
      highlight detail and dark-scene intent. Evaluate whether exposure plus a stable tone curve
      satisfies the requirement before adding local contrast adaptation or its additional history.
    - Apply the camera response once at the shared final display boundary. Keep lighting caches,
      atmospheric radiance and reflection/refraction inputs unexposed; coordinate any exposure-relative
      bloom thresholds with the owned postprocessing tasks. Preserve grading order and final dithering.
    - Integrate through the existing VGE-owned postprocessing and final composition owners;
      scheduling, intermediate targets and final-shader ownership are documented in docs/PBR.Postprocessing.md.
    - Use existing VGE shader, binding, PSO and resource owners with the GLSL 330 baseline; bound
      reduction cost and history storage and avoid synchronous per-frame CPU luminance readback.
      Define startup, camera-cut, teleport, world change, resize/reload and settings-reset behavior.
    - Expose persisted controls through the existing graphics configuration, including a disable/manual
      mode that retains predictable fixed exposure. Support both LumOn modes and current SDR output;
      keep future native-HDR presentation compatible without making it an implementation prerequisite.
    - Validate bright exterior/dark interior transitions, sun entering/leaving the frame, emissive
      objects, underwater views, day/night changes and stationary scenes at varied frame rates.
      Use focused subagent numerical/GPU tests for metering, adaptation, bounds, lifecycle and single
      display conversion; obtain user-run visual acceptance and measure GPU time, bandwidth and memory.

  ☐ Analyze the current PBR MRT textures and evaluate which can be combined together to just use individual channels of the same texture-layer in the G-Buffer array.

  ☐ Profile and optimize cached material-atlas restoration with batched uploads.
    - Establish a matched warm-cache world-load baseline, separating cache reads/decompression,
      CPU tile preparation, scheduling, texture allocation and GPU upload submission/completion.
      The observed 3228 ms synchronous finish with 4944 material and 7460 normal/depth cache hits
      identifies a loading cost, but does not yet prove that individual uploads dominate it.
    - Compare current per-tile uploads with CPU assembly into bounded page or region buffers followed
      by fewer, larger uploads. Measure call count, transferred bytes, peak scratch memory and total
      loading time; avoid trading excessive CPU copying or full-page transfers for fewer calls.
    - Reuse existing texture, streaming and pooled-memory abstractions. Preserve atlas rectangles,
      formats, overrides, neutral/unfilled regions and coherent publication. Keep incremental runtime
      updates efficient and retain the uncached GPU normal/depth baking path where appropriate.
    - Validate mixed cache hits/misses, multiple pages, cancellation, reload and resource retirement
      through subagent-run focused tests. Adopt the measured improvement and document the result;
      distinguish material-atlas savings from the separate unexplained startup delay.

